Interesting — this oidnTest.cpp already has tests like "filter parameters" that look custom (not upstream OIDN). This repo has been modified for the feedback environment. Good — I'll add a "temporal filter" TEST_CASE in the same style.

Note `makeAndCommitDevice()` helper exists. Let me write the temporal test after "filter parameters" test. Insert before `void multiFilter1PerDeviceTest`.

Test plan:
```cpp
TEST_CASE("temporal filter", "[temporal_filter]")
{
  const int W = 129;
  const int H = 65;

  DeviceRef device = makeAndCommitDevice();

  auto color  = makeRandomImage(device, W, H);
  auto output = makeImage(device, W, H);
  auto prevOutput = makeRandomImage(device, W, H);
  auto motion = makeConstImage(device, W, H, 2, DataType::Float32, 0.f);

  FilterRef filter = device.newFilter("RT");
  REQUIRE(bool(filter));

  SECTION("temporal accumulation")
  {
    setFilterImage(filter, "color", color);
    setFilterImage(filter, "output", output);
    setFilterImage(filter, "prevOutput", prevOutput);
    setFilterImage(filter, "motion", motion);
    filter.set("hdr", true);
    filter.set("temporalBlend", 0.5f);
    REQUIRE(filter.get<float>("temporalBlend") == 0.5f);
    filter.commit();
    REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::None);

    filter.execute();
    REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::None);
    REQUIRE(isBetween(output, 0.f, 1.0f)); // output sanity check
  }

  SECTION("temporal accumulation with aux images")
  {
    auto albedo = makeConstImage(device, W, H, 3, DataType::Float32, 0.5f);
    auto normal = makeConstImage(device, W, H, 3, DataType::Float32, 0.5f);
    auto prevAlbedo = makeConstImage(device, W, H, 3, DataType::Float32, 0.5f);
    auto prevNormal = makeConstImage(device, W, H, 3, DataType::Float32, 0.5f);
    ... set all, commit, execute, sanity check
  }

  SECTION("incomplete temporal parameters")
  {
    setFilterImage(filter, "color", color);
    setFilterImage(filter, "output", output);
    setFilterImage(filter, "prevOutput", prevOutput); // motion missing
    filter.commit();
    REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::InvalidOperation);
  }

  SECTION("invalid temporal blend")
  {
    filter.set("temporalBlend", 1.5f);
    REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::InvalidArgument);
  }

  SECTION("output overlapping history")
  {
    // output cannot overlap prevOutput
    setFilterImage(filter, "color", color);
    setFilterImage(filter, "output", prevOutput);  // same image as history
    setFilterImage(filter, "prevOutput", prevOutput);
    setFilterImage(filter, "motion", motion);
    filter.commit();
    REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::InvalidOperation);
  }

  SECTION("single-frame compatibility")
  {
    // without temporal images the filter must behave exactly as before
    setFilterImage(filter, "color", color);
    setFilterImage(filter, "output", output);
    filter.set("hdr", true);
    filter.commit();
    REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::None);
    filter.execute();
    REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::None);
    REQUIRE(isBetween(output, 0.f, 1.0f));
  }
}
```

Wait: error handling — commit failure: does commit throw or set device error? In the C++ API (oidn.hpp), exceptions from filter are caught and stored as device error. Existing test does `filter.commit(); REQUIRE(device.getError() == Error::InvalidOperation);`? Check existing pattern: line 678 "Try committing the filter without setting any images / filter.commit();" then presumably REQUIRE(device.getError() == ...). Let me check lines 676-690.

Also isBetween(output, 0.1, 1.0) used for hdr const 0.5 input. For random inputs in [0,1], output in [0,1] plausible; temporal blend of two [0,1] images also in [0,1]. Use 0.f..1.f.

One concern: `makeConstImage(device, W, H, 2, DataType::Float32, 0.f)` — ImageBuffer supports C=2? Format Float2 exists. getFormat for 2 channels → Format::Float2. Should be fine.

Also `filter.set("temporalBlend", 1.5f)` → setFloat throws InvalidArgument synchronously? In api.cpp, oidnSetFilterFloat wraps in try/catch storing device error. Yes, existing test relies on that pattern.

Check how commit error is verified in existing test (line ~677).

